Make the CalculiX iterative-solver convergence criterion reachable.

ccx's iterative solvers (*STATIC, SOLVER=ITERATIVE CHOLESKY / ITERATIVE
SCALING) are the only ones whose memory fits a large periodic cell. On a
167k-equation RVE: 370 MB against SPOOLES' 1275 MB, and 21 s against 23 s.

But out of the box they return the wrong answer, and quietly. The convergence
test in pcgsolver.c is

    if (k!=1 && (ram<=c1*qam)) break;      /* c1 = 0.005 */

so the iteration stops once the residual max-norm falls to 0.5% of the mean
load. On an RVE with a ~70x phase contrast that leaves the effective modulus
0.15% below the direct-solver answer on the same deck -- a number that looks
entirely reasonable on its own.

c1 is a local constant that no input deck reaches. The `eps` argument threaded
down from preiter.c LOOKS like the tolerance and is documented as "required
accuracy" in pcgsolver.c, but it is never read: PCG and CG overwrite it with
the final residual on exit and return it. Setting it does nothing, which is
worth knowing before spending an afternoon on it.

This patch reads c1 from CCX_ITER_TOL in both solvers, and the iteration cap
from CCX_ITER_MAXIT in preiter.c, echoing both. With neither variable set the
behaviour is byte-for-byte the stock behaviour.

Measured on a 40973-element (167306-equation) brine RVE, against SPOOLES on
the identical deck:

    CCX_ITER_TOL   iters   wall    peak     E_eff error   equilibrium gap
    0.005 (stock)     70   14.0s   370 MB      -0.1477%        1.8e-03
    1e-3              94   14.6s   370 MB      +0.0133%        3.3e-06
    1e-4             231   17.7s   370 MB      +0.0007%        6.0e-06
    1e-5             338   20.9s   370 MB      -0.0000%        1.8e-07
    1e-6             453   23.4s   370 MB      +0.0000%        4.2e-08
    SPOOLES (direct)   -   23.2s  1275 MB     (reference)      7.4e-08

1e-5 is the recommended setting: it matches the direct solver to five decimal
places at a third of the memory and no more wall time. Memory is flat in the
tolerance, as it must be for a conjugate-gradient method -- the cost of
tightening is iterations, not storage.

The last column is the check that survives into production. It is the
disagreement between the volume-averaged stress and the reference-point
reaction -- two independent measurements of the same macroscopic stress -- and
it tracks the true error without needing a reference solve, so it remains
available on a cell far too large to solve directly even once. SpaX_PostProcess
records it per RVE as `equilibrium_gap`.

Apply against a CalculiX 2.23 source tree (this machine has no `patch`; git
apply does the same job):

    cd <ccx_2.23>
    git apply -p0 0001-iterative-tolerance.patch     # or: patch -p0 < ...
    cd src && make -j8

Verified: applying this to pristine 2.23 sources reproduces the built binary's
sources exactly.

--- src/preiter.c
+++ src/preiter.c
@@ -26,9 +26,25 @@
   ITG precFlg,niter=5000000,ndim,i,j,k,ier,*icol=NULL,*irow=NULL,
     *irow_save=NULL,*icol_save=NULL;
   double eps=1.e-4,*u=NULL,*au=NULL;
+  char *spax_env;
 
   if(*neq==0) return;
 
+  /* SPAX: `eps` is NOT the convergence tolerance despite its name and the
+     comment in pcgsolver.c -- it is overwritten with the final residual on
+     exit and returned. The actual criterion is c1 in pcgsolver.c, patched
+     there to read CCX_ITER_TOL. Only the iteration cap is a genuine input
+     here, and raising it is what a tightened tolerance needs. Echo both so the
+     log records what was used. */
+  if((spax_env=getenv("CCX_ITER_MAXIT"))!=NULL){
+    niter=atoi(spax_env);
+  }
+  {
+    char *tol=getenv("CCX_ITER_TOL");
+    printf(" iterative solver: tolerance %s, iteration cap %" ITGFORMAT "\n",
+           tol!=NULL?tol:"0.005 (default)",niter);
+  }
+
   /* icol(i) = # subdiagonal nonzeros in column i (i=1,neq)
      irow(i) = row number of entry i in au (i=1,nzs)
      ad(i) = diagonal term in column i of the matrix
--- src/pcgsolver.c
